  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nMostly Dry This Week...The region will be under the influence of a \nridge of high pressure through at least Friday, keeping conditions \ndry under mostly sunny skies. The previously mentioned small chances \nfor precip on Wednesday associated from a cold front to the north \nhave decreased to a less than 10% chance of rain (ensembles and NBM) \ndue to the dominant dry air from the prevailing high pressure. If \nanything, the forecast area would see an increase in clouds on \nWednesday. Otherwise, guidance is indicating the next chance for \nrain for sometime this weekend as a mid-level trough and surface low \neject northeast from the Middle Rockies. However, timing and \nplacement is not certain with the evolution of this system. At this \npoint, probabilities for 0.10 inches of rain have increased slightly \nto up to 25% by Sunday evening.\n\nFog Potential...The prevailing high pressure combined with nighttime \ncooling and light winds will result in the potential for at least \npatchy ground fog each night/morning from Monday night through at \nleast Wednesday night. A couple of these nights could have locally \ndense fog.\n\nTemperatures...This weather pattern will keep temperatures above \nnormal through the week and into the weekend with highs mainly in \nthe 60s each day. A few spots could see highs in the low 70s \nstarting on Wednesday. Overnight lows will also be above normal with \nvalues mainly in the 40s. Monday night will be the coldest night \nwith the surface high centered overhead, causing locations across \nthe north to see values in the 30s.",
